{"name":"Fife","hex":"#a9a5c2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Fife","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A traditional county of Scotland succeeded by Fife Region in 1975, situated between the Firth of Tay and the Firth of Forth, with landward boundaries to Perth and Kinross and Clackmannanshire.","A council area of Scotland, one of 32 created in 1996."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Fife"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fife","blocks":[{"text":"Fife is a council area and lieutenancy area in Scotland. A peninsula, it is bordered by the Firth of Tay to the north, the North Sea to the east, the Firth of Forth to the south, Clackmannanshire to the south-west, and Perth and Kinross to the west. The largest settlement is the city of Dunfermline, and the administrative centre is Glenrothes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Crail%2C_Fife%2C_Scotland.jpg/330px-Crail%2C_Fife%2C_Scotland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fife"},{"title":"Fife (instrument)","blocks":[{"text":"A fife is a small, high-pitched, transverse aerophone, that is similar to the piccolo. The fife originated in medieval Europe and is often used in fife and drum corps, military units, and marching bands. Someone who plays the fife is called a fifer. The word fife comes from the German Pfeife, meaning pipe, which comes from the Latin word pipare."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Fife-wooden%2C_civil_war_era.jpeg/330px-Fife-wooden%2C_civil_war_era.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fife_(instrument)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Dunfermline_Abbey_-_geograph.org.uk_-_103053.jpg/250px-Dunfermline_Abbey_-_geograph.org.uk_-_103053.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_conte